.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH birthd 1  "birthd 0.10.6" 
.SH NAME
birthd \- Build daemon that watches and rebuilds Rust projects
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBbirthd\fR [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
birthd — build daemon that watches Rust projects and rebuilds on change
.PP
birthd scans for .birth.toml files, sets up filesystem watchers, and
automatically rebuilds and reinstalls binaries when source files change.
.PP
When a watched path emits a create, modify, or remove event, the owning
project is queued and rebuilt once its configured debounce window
(default 500 ms) has elapsed with no further events. The default build
is \fBcargo build \-\-release\fR through
.BR baby 's
build_and_install pipeline (including boar RAM delegation and locksmithd
coordination); a custom build command from the config file runs instead
when one is set. After a successful build the configured systemd service,
if any, is restarted (via sudo when the config requests it).
.PP
birthd is a foreground daemon: it does not fork. It writes its PID file
on startup and removes it on graceful shutdown.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
\fBSIGTERM\fR
Graceful shutdown: finish the current loop iteration, remove the PID
file, and exit.
.TP
\fBSIGINT\fR
Graceful shutdown, as for SIGTERM.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.PP
birthd is configured by .birth.toml files. The current directory's
.birth.toml is loaded first, then every *.toml file in the user and
system config directories (see FILES). Unparseable files are skipped
with a warning. Each file describes one project:
.PP
.RS
.nf
project = "mytool"            # binary name (required)
watch = ["src", "Cargo.toml"] # paths to watch (relative to the config file)
build = "cargo build --release"  # default
install = "/usr/local/bin"    # install directory (default /usr/local/bin)
restart = "mytool.service"    # optional systemd unit to restart
debounce_ms = 500             # default 500
strip = false                 # strip symbols before installing
backup = false                # back up the existing binary
sudo = false                  # use sudo for install/restart
user = false                  # install to ~/.local/bin instead
.fi
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.SH FILES
.TP
.I .birth.toml
Project configuration in the current working directory.
.TP
.I ~/.config/birth.d/*.toml
User\-level project configurations ($XDG_CONFIG_HOME/birth.d if set).
.TP
.I /etc/birth.d/*.toml
System\-level project configurations.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/birthd.pid
Daemon PID file (fallback: /tmp/birthd.pid).
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/birthd.log
Daemon log file (fallback: ~/.local/state/birthd.log, then /tmp).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBXDG_RUNTIME_DIR\fR, \fBXDG_STATE_HOME\fR, \fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR
Locate the PID file, log file, and user config directory.
.TP
\fBRUST_LOG\fR
Log filter for the log file (default: info).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Clean shutdown.
.TP
1
Startup failure (e.g. logging or PID file setup failed).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR baby (1),
.BR birthctl (1),
.BR boar (1)
.SH VERSION
v0.10.6
